 ## Overview
-3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) is a differentiable, point-based scene representation that replaces the implicit multi-layer perceptron (MLP) and ray-marched volume integration of NeRF with an explicit collection of 3D anisotropic Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. It enables real-time synthesis of high-fidelity novel views via a GPU-friendly tile-based rasterizer that depth-sorts and alpha-composites 2D projections of these Gaussians [S2, S5, unofficial]. Unlike NeRF, which infers density and color at arbitrary points, 3DGS optimizes the parameters of a fixed set of Gaussian primitives (position, covariance, opacity, and spherical harmonic coefficients) to minimize photometric loss, using adaptive density control to adjust the number of primitives during training [S3, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) is a differentiable, point-based scene representation that replaces the implicit multi-layer perceptron (MLP) and ray-marched volume integration of NeRF with an explicit collection of 3D anisotropic Gaussians [S3, unofficial]. It enables real-time synthesis of high-fidelity novel views via a GPU-friendly tile-based rasterizer that depth-sorts and alpha-composites 2D projections of these Gaussians [S2, unofficial][S5]. Unlike NeRF, which infers density and color at arbitrary points, 3DGS optimizes the parameters of a fixed set of Gaussian primitives (position, covariance, opacity, and spherical harmonic coefficients) to minimize photometric loss, using adaptive density control to adjust the number of primitives during training [S3, unofficial].
 
