 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(N)$ per pixel where $N$ is the number of Gaussians contributing to the pixel, but accelerated by tiling and culling. Training is dominated by the rasterization and backpropagation steps, scaling with the number of Gaussians and image resolution.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Each Gaussian stores $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ (12 bytes), $q$ (16 bytes), $s$ (12 bytes), $\alpha$ (4 bytes), and SH coefficients (192 bytes for degree 3), totaling ~236 bytes per Gaussian.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is $O(N)$ per pixel where $N$ is the number of Gaussians contributing to the pixel, but accelerated by tiling and culling [G]. Training is dominated by the rasterization and backpropagation steps, scaling with the number of Gaussians and image resolution [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of Gaussians [G]. Each Gaussian stores $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ (12 bytes), $q$ (16 bytes), $s$ (12 bytes), $\alpha$ (4 bytes), and SH coefficients (192 bytes for degree 3), totaling ~236 bytes per Gaussian [G].
 *   **Performance:** Real-time rendering (100–300 FPS at 1080p) is achievable on modern GPUs due to the tile-based rasterizer [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High-fidelity rendering, real-time performance, explicit representation allows for easy editing and manipulation.
-*   **Limitations:** Popping artifacts due to depth sorting, pre-classification artifacts, and heuristic densification can lead to redundancy. Not inherently designed for volumetric media, leading to inaccuracies in TF evaluation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Ideal for static scene reconstruction and novel view synthesis where real-time performance and high visual quality are required.
+*   **Strengths:** High-fidelity rendering, real-time performance, explicit representation allows for easy editing and manipulation [G].
+*   **Limitations:** Popping artifacts due to depth sorting, pre-classification artifacts, and heuristic densification can lead to redundancy [S2, S3, unofficial]. Not inherently designed for volumetric media, leading to inaccuracies in TF evaluation [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Ideal for static scene reconstruction and novel view synthesis where real-time performance and high visual quality are required [G].
 *   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for scenes with complex volumetric effects (e.g., smoke, fire) without modifications, as it does not model volumetric ray integration [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 as a state-of-the-art reconstruction approach [S1]. It represents scenes using 3D Gaussians, serving as a baseline for many subsequent methods despite not being specifically designed for volumetric media [S1]. The method leverages hardware rasterization for efficient rendering, replacing the implicit MLPs of NeRF with explicit Gaussian primitives [S2, S3, unofficial]. It has become a foundational technique in computer graphics, inspiring numerous variants for dynamic scenes, compression, and volumetric rendering [S5, unofficial].
